{
  "_comment": [
    "Curated cross-linguistic interference traps for a learner whose L1s are",
    "English and Italian. Research 06 established these matter more than they",
    "look: for close language pairs the documented failure mode is that",
    "similarity helps at the start and hurts later, producing fossilised errors.",
    "",
    "Two uses, and only two:",
    "  1. force-include. These lemmas are LOW FREQUENCY by nature, so a",
    "     frequency-ranked lemma cut drops them. Without force-inclusion the",
    "     product cannot teach the thing it is best placed to teach.",
    "  2. as the WRONG ANSWER in a multiple-choice recall prompt. The gloss",
    "     meta-analysis found multiple choice the most effective format, and the",
    "     Involvement Load Hypothesis found `evaluation` the highest-value",
    "     component. A false friend as the distractor is both at once.",
    "",
    "Not a headline feature. Research 06 argued against that: base rates are low",
    "(you must actually type 'butter' for `burro` to fire) and interference errors",
    "only exist once the USER produces Spanish, which is Mode E. Ship it as",
    "cold-start priors plus a high-precision interrupt.",
    "",
    "`trap` is what the learner will wrongly assume it means.",
    "`want` is the Spanish word they actually needed."
  ],
